     Wish                      OS-9 USERS MANUAL                       Wish     NNAAMMEE               Wish - windowing user-friendly shell-overlay      SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS                usage  : wish -[afp?]                                        options:      -a no file Attributes window                                 -f no virtual F-key labels                         -p display current directory                                 -? print help    (this text)             DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN                                           _O_V_E_R_V_I_E_W          WiSH,  the  Windowing  Shell, is a powerful, easy to use and very          user  friendly  interface  to  OS-9.  It must be understood as an          additional  layer  on  top of microware's shell  ( ..or any other          line oriented command interpreter).                                   _C_O_M_M_A_N_D_L_I_N_E _O_P_T_I_O_N_S          WiSH  has  several commandline options which allows you to select          several run-time options. They are:        -f      WiSH detects the presence of an HP-Terminal by the presence                of  the  "xs"-flag in the termcap entry. So, if this flag is               NOT  present,  WiSH  emulates the function key labels in the               last  line of the screen.  If this behaviour is not desired,               use  the -f switch to run WiSH without emulated function key               labels.         -p      Normally the first file displayed in the file window is the                "..",  the  directory  above  the current directory. This is               for  easy  walking  through the directory tree. Although the               current  directory, ".", is not displayed, it is present and               included  in  the  entry  count in the upper right corner of               the  file  window.  If  you  WiSH  to  display  the  current               directory too, use the -p switch to turn this option on.         -a      If you dont like to contiuously have the line showing the                current  attributes  displayed,  you  can switch this off by               using  the -a switch.  As a side effect, you will be able to               use two more screen lines for the file window display.         -?      Displays the usage information and version of WiSH        When WiSH starts, the crt becomed divided into to areas:        -   the   top  line  normally  shows  the  logo  and  the  copyright       information  but  in  case  of  an  error,  the error information is       displayed there until the next key is pressed.         -  the  second  line  is  a  "normal"  shell  input  commandline  80       characters  wide.  Any  alphanumeric  input typed at the keyboard is       displayed  here  and  after pressing <return> executed by a "normal"       shell.         -  the  middle  part  of  the  screen becomes your "window" into the       currently  selected  directory - it's called the file window. In the       first  line  of  the  file window the full pathname of the currently     Page 1                                  Printed (16 Sep 1990 12:05:54)     Wish                      OS-9 USERS MANUAL                       Wish       selected  is  displayed,  together  with the number of files in this       directory  (don't  become  confused by the number of files, remember       directories  are  just  files  too  !!).   Then  the directories are       displayed followed by the files in the directory.         -  the  lower  part of the screen is the status window. Here are the       attributes of the currently selected file displayed.                                 _U_S_I_N_G _F_U_N_C_T_I_O_N _K_E_Y_S       WiSH  is  completely  controllable  by  the  terminals special keys,       which  are  the  cursor  keys  (up,  down,  right and left), special       function  keys  (insert  line, delete line, previous, next etc.) and       function  keys  (f1....f10).   The  latter  can  have  function  key       labels,  such  as  all Hewlett-Packard Terminals.  If a Terminal has       function  keys, but no labels, WiSH simulates 10 function key labels       in  the  last  line  of  the  terminal (selectable via a commandline       option).                                  _- _O_R _C_O_N_T_R_O_L _C_O_D_E_S       Because  there  are still many not so intelligent "old" terminals in       the  field,  every  function  key  has  an  Emacs-style control code       equivalent,  i.e.  to  scroll  the file window to the next page, you       can  either  use  the function key labled "NEXT" (on many terminals)       or  alternatively  you  can  use  the  Emacs-style  "^V" (Control-V,       generated  by  pressing the terminal's control- key and the letter-V       key simultaneously).                                _F_U_L_L _S_C_R_E_E_N _O_P_E_R_A_T_I_O_N       With  the  cursor  keys,  the  user  moves  a  "cursor",  which is a       highlighted  bar,  over  the filenames in the file window. If a file       is  made  the  current  selected  file  by placing the cursor on it,       various  operations  can  be  done  on  it,  i.e.  if  the file is a       directory,  and  the user hits return, a change to that directory is       done.  If the file is an executable file, it is either executed as a       binary  executable  or as a shell script. If the file is a data file       it  is  displayed  by  the  built-in  pager  - much more options are       available.   If  there  are  more  files  than fitting into the file       window, this window can be easily scrolled by the user.                                 _F_I_L_E_S _C_A_N _B_E _M_A_R_K_E_D       Files  can  be  marked  in  the  file  window  and  expanded  in the       commandline  by the special wildcard character "%" -  so if you mark       several  files  in the file window, and type "del % <RETURN>" in the       commandline, all selected files will be deleted.         The  current  selected  filename  can be echoed onto the commandline       for further processing by some other OS-9 commands.                                     _I_N_S_T_A_L_L_A_T_I_O_N       WiSH  was  designed  for  Microware's  OS-9/68000  operating system.       There  is  no  need for any special-purpose add-on's in order to run       WiSH.         First  you  have  to  copy  WiSH from the distribution disk into the       subdirectory   where   your   executable   programs   reside,   i.e.       "/h0/CMDS".         Then  eventually  you have to design your own termcap entry for your     Page 2                                  Printed (16 Sep 1990 12:05:57)     Wish                      OS-9 USERS MANUAL                       Wish       specific terminal type you want WiSH to run on.         NOTE:  in  the  current  version,  ALL  codes  sent  by  the special       function  keys  must  start  with  an  ESC  (1b  hex) in order to be       recognized by WiSH.          li              number of lines on screen        co              number of columns on screen        up              cursor up        cl              clear screen        cm              cursor positioning        ce              clear to end of line        so              start standout mode (inverse on)        se              standout mode end (inverse off)        us              start underline mode (underline on)        ue              underline mode end (underline off)        as              alternate character set start        ae              alternate character set end        xs              standout not erased by overwrite -                        this terminal is an Hewlett-Packard                        terminal and is handled specially        pc              pad character (if necessary)        bt              backward tabulator        k1              string sent by function key 1        k2              string sent by function key 2        k3              string sent by function key 3        k4              string sent by function key 4        k5              string sent by function key 5        k6              string sent by function key 6        k7              string sent by function key 7        k8              string sent by function key 8        k9              string sent by function key 9        k0              string sent by function key 10        kb              backspace        kC              clear screen        kD              delete character        kd              cursor down        kE              clear to end of line        kH              home down / end of screen        kh              home up        kL              delete line        kl              cursor left        kN              next page        kP              previous page        kr              cursor right        ku              cursor up                                _T_E_R_M_C_A_P__E_X_A_M_P_L_E     Page 3                                  Printed (16 Sep 1990 12:05:58)     Wish                      OS-9 USERS MANUAL                       Wish     Here  is  an example termcap-entry for your reference - it is an entry     for an Hewlett-Packard Terminal HP2392A in 80-column mode.          hp|hp2392|hewlett-packard 2392a:                 :ae=^O:al=EL:am:as=^N:bs:bt=Ei:                 :cd=EJ:ce=EK:ch=E&a%dC:cl=EHEJ:co#80:                 :cm=E&a%dy%dC:cv=E&a%dY:                 :da:db:dc=EP:dl=EM:do=EB:                 :ei=ER:ho=EH:im=EQ:                 :kb=^H:kd=EB:kh=Eh:                 :kl=ED:kr=EC:ku=EA:                 :ke=E&s0A:ks=E&s1A:                 :kS=EJ:kD=EP:kL=EM:                 :kC=EJ:kE=EK:kI=EQ:                 :kA=EL:kH=EF:kN=EU:                 :kP=EV:kF=ET:kR=ES:                 :k1=Ep:k2=Eq:k3=Er:k4=Es:                 :k5=Et:k6=Eu:k7=Ev:k8=Ew:                 :li#24:mb=E&dA:me=E&d@:mh=E&dH:                 :mi:ml=El:mu=Em:nd=EC:                 :so=E&dB:se=E&d@:                 :ue=E&d@:up=EA:us=E&dD:xs:xo:      For  further  reference  to  termcap and how to design entries for the     termcap  database,  see:  OS-9/68000  C  Compilers  User's Manual, The     Termcap Library, Pages 5-1 ... 5-16.                                 _C_O_M_M_A_N_D_S__a_n_d__K_E_Y_S     This  chapter  explains what and how commands are mapped to which keys     on  your  keyboard.  The  labels  on the terminal you are using may be     different  from the keys as described below. If in doubt, consult your     OS-9  system guru, the supplier of WiSH or the author of WiSH directly     - in that order.       _o_p_i_n_i_o_n__i_s__v_e_r_y__w_e_l_c_o_m_e__!_!_!        UP-arrow        ^P      moving the cursor up one filename       DOWN-arrow      ^N      moving the cursor down one filename       LEFT-arrow      ^B      moving the cursor left one filename       RIGHT-arrow     ^F      moving the cursor right one filename       HOME                    moving the cursor to the upper left filename       HOME-DOWN               moving the cursor to the lower right filename       TAB             ^F      moving the cursor right one filename       BACK-TAB        ^B      moving the cursor left one filename       NEXT            ^Z      scrolling to the next file-window page       PREV            ^V      scrolling to the previous file-window page     Page 4                                  Printed (16 Sep 1990 12:06:01)     Wish                      OS-9 USERS MANUAL                       Wish       F8 or F10       ^D      exit WiSH and return to OS-9                       ^L      refresh display screen       F1              ^W      copy current selected filename into commandline       F2              ^A      mark current filename       F3              ^U      un-mark all files on all pages       BACKSPACE       ^H      delete last entered character       DEL                     delete last entered character       DEL CHAR                delete last entered character       CLR LINE                delete command line       DEL LINE                delete command line                             _S_T_A_R_T_U_P__A_N_D__O_P_E_R_A_T_I_O_N     Any  non-control  character  typed  is displayed on the commandline in     the   commandline  window on the second line of the crt. The exception     to  this are the special control codes <CR> = Carriage Return and <BS>     =  Backspace.   A  command  string  entered  into  the command line is     terminated  by  typing  <CR>, and then handed to OS-9's system() call,     to  execute it. <BS> is used to delete the last entered character from     the commandline.       With  the exception of the commands "chd", "cd" and "chx", every other     command is handled by OS-9.       "chd"  and  "cd" are used to change the current data directory for the     user while "chx" is used to change the current execution directory.       A  special purpose wildcard character is recognized and expanded after     pressing  <CR>,  this  is  the character "%" (percent). This character     expands  to  any marked files in the file-window(s) and gives the user     a  new  powerful  function  for example to copy files, for wich normal     wildcarding  is not easy to determine. To go on with the copy-example,     simply  enter  a  command  like:  "copy  %  -w=/h0/destdir", and every     marked  file  from  your  current  directory  will  be  copied  to the     directory /h0/destdir.                              _U_S_A_G_E__O_F__T_H_E__F_I_L_E__W_I_N_D_O_W     While  you  are maneuvering with the highlighting bar through the file     window,  you are able to do the most natural commands depending on the     type  of  file  to  the  current  selected  (highlighted) file by just     pressing  <CR>  (for  this  function  to work, the commandline MUST be     empty, otherwise the commandline  is terminated and executed !).       This means, that if your file is       - an executable binary program, it is executed         (an "e" attribute must set and the first 2 bytes in the file          must be 4afc hex)       - a shell script, it is interpreted by the shell selected by the      Page 5                                  Printed (16 Sep 1990 12:06:03)     Wish                      OS-9 USERS MANUAL                       Wish        SHELL or WSHELL environment variable         (an "e" attribute must be set and the first two bytes in the          file must be different from 4afc hex)       - a data file, it is displayed using the internal pager         (no "e" attribute must be set but an "r" attribute)       - a directory, the current directory is changed to that         (the "d" attribute must be set)      The  current  selected  file can be marked (and unmarked again) by the     mark function key or it's equivalent control code.       All marked files can be unmarked by the unmark function key.       The  highlighted filename can be "transported" to the commandline with     the echo function key.       In  the  last  line of the display, the file attributes of the current     highlighted  file are displayed (this can be disabled by a commandline     option).  from left to right this are:      Siz: - The file size in bytes      Atr: - The attributes (dsewrewr), where a non-existent attribute is             displayed using a dash ("-").       Usr: - The user number of the owner of the file      Grp: - The group number of the owner of the file      Crd: - The creation date of the file      Wrd: - The date, the last time the file was written to      CCAAVVEEAATTSS      