hich  i liked to use a nice          horizontal  dash.  The  terminal itself has the ability to switch          his  character sets by sending the ASCII codes "SI" and "SO". The          representation  of  the  horizontal  dash in ASCII is ";". So, if          you  set  WISHDELIM  to  this  character  by  issuing the command          "setenv  WISHDELIM ;", WiSH uses now the graphical representation          of  that  characters  instead  of  the default "=" to delimit the          three horizontal parts of the screen.            Because  WiSH  detects  shell-script  files  and tries to execute          them, WiSH must know, which shell to use for this purpose.            Normally,  the  environment  variable  "SHELL"  is  set, when you          logon  into  your  system before the shell starts up.  But if you          use  WiSH  as  your  logon-shell, this variable is set to "WiSH",          but   WiSH   is   (in   the   moment..)   not   able  to  execute          shell-scripts.            Because  of  this,  if  WiSH  tries to execute a shell-script, it          first  looks at the environment variable "WSHELL" to get the name          of  the  shell  suitable  for  executing  scrips.  If "WSHELL" is          undefined, WiSH uses "SHELL".            _H_O_M_E__-__u_s_e_r_s__h_o_m_e_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y           WiSH  uses this environment variable for the execution of a "chd"          command  with no arguments to change the current directory to the          users homedirectory.            entry of the users terminal      SSEEEE AALLSSOO               Using Professional OS-9, Microware Systems Corporation:      Page 7                                  Printed (16 Sep 1990 12:06:08)     Wish                      OS-9 USERS MANUAL                       Wish           - The Shell     (pp. 5-1)           - Shell Utility (pp. 8-100)          CCOOPPYYRRIIGGHHTT                 WiSH is (